summary:
- topic: Student Representative Report
  text: The student representative provided updates on high school athletics playoffs,
    PSAT prep sessions, a mental health family night, and various elementary school
    activities like a costume swap and flu clinics. The mental health family night
    at the high school involved community partners and aimed to share resources and
    support for teen mental health.
- topic: Superintendent's Report Highlights
  text: The Superintendent celebrated October as National Principals Month, acknowledging
    the challenging yet rewarding work of principals and assistant principals. She
    also praised the successful Education Justice Conference, noting positive feedback
    from educators and plans to build on its momentum.
- topic: Facilities and Community Support
  text: Harold Blaney was formally introduced as the new Director of Buildings and
    Grounds, recognized for his technical expertise and commitment to customer service.
    The Superintendent also highlighted the 'Season of Giving' initiative, encouraging
    community members to support various local efforts like food donations, holiday
    gifts, and winter clothing drives.
- topic: Student Enrollment and Elementary School Demographics
  text: The Superintendent reported a 5% decline in student enrollment since last
    year and presented data revealing significant demographic disparities across elementary
    schools, particularly in the proportions of students of color, economically disadvantaged
    students, and multilingual learners. This data prompted a discussion on the implications
    for educational equity and the need to revisit attendance zones with the help
    of consultants.
- topic: South Portland Middle School Community School Model
  text: The middle school principal and community school director presented on their
    successful Community School model, funded by an ARPA grant. They detailed its
    six key practices, including powerful family engagement, shared leadership, extended
    learning opportunities, and integrated systems of support, showcasing positive
    outcomes like reduced chronic absenteeism and increased grant funding. They advocated
    for district-wide expansion and sustainable funding for the program.
- topic: Greater Portland Health Partnership Data
  text: New data from Greater Portland Health highlighted the significant impact of
    in-school clinics, reporting over 1200 medical visits, 346 dental visits, and
    479 behavioral health visits last year. A notable 59% increase in medical visits
    and high utilization by multilingual learners underscored the critical role these
    services play in addressing fundamental student needs.
- topic: Policy Approvals and Rescissions
  text: The board approved the consent agenda, which included personnel elections,
    various community donations, and the rescission of several outdated library policies
    (PolicyIJJ-R, PolicyIJNC, PolicyIJNCA, PolicyIJNCA-E1) due to duplication, while
    assuring continued strong protection of library resources. Additionally, new policies
    regarding Automated External Defibrillators (AEDs), Job Descriptions, and Suspension
    of Students were approved.
- topic: Board Member Election Announcement
  text: During the open audience of citizens, Board Chair Rosemarie DeAngelis announced
    her re-election campaign and encouraged public support for Adrian Dowling as a
    write-in candidate for District Five, emphasizing his qualifications and commitment
    to a full term.
