  topic: Community School Framework at South Portland Middle School Presentation
  desc: Superintendent Atkinson Duina invited Dr. Stern and Liana Popkin to present
    on the Community School framework at South Portland Middle School, highlighting
    its successful work.
  children:
  - seconds: 1598
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Rebecca Stern, Principal of the middle school, and Liana Popkin, Community
      School Director, introduced their presentation on the Community School model.
      They explained that community schools leverage community assets to serve needs,
      and shared their Needs and Assets report, which involved extensive community
      input.
  - seconds: 1634
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Rebecca Stern defined a community school as a model where schools and communities
      collaborate to enhance learning, opportunities, and equity, comparing it to
      a smartphone's versatility. She noted that South Portland Middle School is the
      only middle school in Maine to adopt this national model.
  - seconds: 1776
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Liana Popkin emphasized that community schools offer hyperlocal solutions,
      improve student outcomes, are cost-effective, and foster stronger school-family
      connections. She highlighted their focus on supporting the whole child and strengthening
      community bonds.
  - seconds: 1821
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Popkin provided a brief history, noting the school received $664,000 in
      ARPA funding in 2022. An advisory board helped identify chronic absenteeism
      and sense of belonging as key priorities, leading to a thorough Needs and Assets
      assessment.
  - seconds: 1904
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Popkin explained that the Needs and Assets assessment involved 105 focus
      group participants and 800 survey results, leading to targeted summaries shared
      with the community. This process ensured that community feedback directly informed
      school goals and priorities.
  - seconds: 1939
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Stern detailed how the school's goals for the year are directly driven by
      the Needs and Assets report, focusing not only on academic goals but also on
      attendance, family/community engagement, and fostering a sense of belonging.
      She outlined six key practices of community schools.
  - seconds: 1974
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Stern discussed 'powerful student and family engagement,' explaining a revamped
      conference schedule to allow for more regular and informal interactions. She
      noted a 250% increase in PTO attendance and improved communication accessibility
      for families.
  - seconds: 2105
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Popkin highlighted 'shared leadership,' noting that a third of teachers
      participate in instructional or PBIS leadership teams, demonstrating a distributed
      leadership model. She also mentioned the integral role of the community school
      advisory board and the use of targeted summaries to show community feedback
      was valued.
  - seconds: 2151
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Stern spoke about fostering a 'culture of belonging, safety, and care,'
      through developing school-wide traditions with community partners. She mentioned
      partnering with AmeriCorps for Restorative Justice fellows and working with
      the National Research Institute to build staff connections in the new school
      community.
  - seconds: 2190
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Popkin detailed 'extended learning opportunities,' explaining a framework
      for after-school programs that diversify options and reduce barriers like transportation
      and snacks. Partnerships with local nonprofits and district staff have increased
      access to these programs for students.
  - seconds: 2264
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Stern introduced the concept of 'community connected learning,' a draft
      model focusing on increasing the sustainability and intentionality of integrating
      the community into school and vice versa. This involves strategic funding and
      building partnerships to support in-school activities.
  - seconds: 2315
    speaker: Liana Popkin (Community School Director)
    text: Popkin discussed 'integrated systems of support,' emphasizing the optimization
      of existing district resources through intentional teaming. She highlighted
      the school-based health center, a regular mental health team, and sustainable
      structures for food and clothing access, as well as protocols for addressing
      chronic absenteeism.
  - seconds: 2392
    speaker: Rebecca Stern (Principal of the middle school)
    text: Stern presented key outcomes, including 30 after-school clubs offered last
      year and a reduction in chronic absenteeism from 22.2% to 15.8%, surpassing
      the district average. She also noted $134,000 received through grants and donations
      due to increased capacity for fundraising. She concluded by advocating for district-wide
      expansion of the model and a sustainable, permanent funding structure.
  - seconds: 2493
    speaker: Ratliff (Board Member)
